{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Python Modules and Packages\"\n",
        "format: html\n",
        "editor: visual\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Modules\n",
        "\n",
        "A module is a file containing a set of code or a set of functions which can be included in an application. A module could be a file containing a single variable, a function, or a large codebase. This file has a `.py` extension.\n",
        "\n",
        "Examples:\n",
        "\n",
        "-   [Random](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/python-random-function/)\n",
        "-   [Math](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/python-math-module/)\n",
        "-   [Statistics](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/statistics-with-python/)\\\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "A package is a directory containing collections of modules. The directory contains Python modules and also having `__init__.py` file by which the interpreter interprets it as a Package.\n",
        "\n",
        "Examples:\n",
        "\n",
        "-   [Numpy](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/numpy/python-numpy/)\n",
        "-   [Pandas](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/pandas/pandas-tutorial/)\n",
        "-   [Arch](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/data-science/arch-autoregressive-conditional-heteroskedasticity/)\\\n",
        "    \\\n",
        "\n",
        "[Difference Between Module, Package, and Library]{style=\"color:red;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "-   **Module** → A single Python file (`.py` )\n",
        "-   **Package** → A folder containing multiple modules\n",
        "-   **Library** → A collection of packages/modules providing functionality\n",
        "\n",
        "[More](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/what-is-the-difference-between-pythons-module-package-and-library/)\\\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "## Modules\n",
        "\n",
        "### Creating a Module\n",
        "\n",
        "To create a module, we write our code and save that in a file with`.py` extension.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Example:** Let's create a `calc.py` in which we define two functions, one add and another subtract."
      ],
      "id": "4aeb8fdb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# calc.py\n",
        "def add(x, y):\n",
        "    return (x+y)\n",
        "\n",
        "def subtract(x, y):\n",
        "    return (x-y)"
      ],
      "id": "0fb4bed9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This is all that is required to create a module.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Importing a Module\n",
        "\n",
        "Modules can be used in another file using the `import` statement. When Python sees an import, it loads the module if it exists in the interpreter’s search path.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Example:** Here, we are importing the **calc** that we created earlier to perform add operation."
      ],
      "id": "89a52838"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import calc\n",
        "\n",
        "print(calc.add(10,2))"
      ],
      "id": "26f0f932",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Built-in Modules\n",
        "\n",
        "Some common built-in Python modules include `math`, `datetime`, `os`, `sys`, `random`, `statistics`, `collections`, `json`, and `re`. These built-in modules can also be imported using the `import` statement, just as we did with the user-defined module above. Let us consider how functions are imported from a module.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Import Functions from a Module\n",
        "\n",
        "**01. Import One or More Specific Functions from a Module**\n",
        "\n",
        "This allows importing specific functions, classes, or variables rather than the whole module."
      ],
      "id": "0380ffa6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "\n",
        "from random import randint, shuffle\n",
        "\n",
        "fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"mango\", \"orange\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(randint(5, 20)) # it returns a random integer number between [5, 20] inclusive\n",
        "print(shuffle(fruits)) # changes the order of elements in a list randomly\n",
        "print(choice(fruits)) # selects one random element from a given list"
      ],
      "id": "4aeae22e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import random\n",
        "from random import randint, shuffle\n",
        "\n",
        "fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"mango\", \"orange\"]\n",
        "print(random.choice(fruits))"
      ],
      "id": "35c3658b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Explanation:** Only sqrt and factorial are brought into the local namespace, so the prefix random. is not required for those functions. But when we use the choice function, it returns an error message without the prefix random.\\\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "**02. Import All Functions**\n",
        "\n",
        "This imports everything from a module into the current namespace."
      ],
      "id": "b167f4e7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from statistics import * \n",
        "\n",
        "ages = [20, 20, 4, 24, 25, 22, 26, 20, 23, 22, 26]\n",
        "print(mean(ages))       \n",
        "print(median(ages))     \n",
        "print(mode(ages))       \n",
        "print(stdev(ages))      "
      ],
      "id": "266479a9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Explanation: Every public name of `statistics` becomes directly accessible. To check what functions the module has got, we can use help(statistics), or dir(statistics)."
      ],
      "id": "6493c07d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "dir(statistics)"
      ],
      "id": "6af88f28",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "**03. Import With Alias**\n",
        "\n",
        "When we import we can also rename the name of the function."
      ],
      "id": "ea97cca6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from math import pi as  P\n",
        "\n",
        "print(P) "
      ],
      "id": "050c557d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "\\\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "## Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "### Installing and Importing Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "Unlike modules, packages cannot always be imported directly; they must first be installed."
      ],
      "id": "06fe5a4a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "\n",
        "import seaborn"
      ],
      "id": "2b4e42c5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "!pip install seaborn\n",
        "\n",
        "import seaborn"
      ],
      "id": "fe0e681f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "!pip install seaborn\n",
        "\n",
        "import seaborn as sn\n",
        "sn.lineplot(\n",
        "    x=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n",
        "    y=[10, 15, 12, 18, 20]\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "22da3600",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### [Comparison with R]{style=\"color:blue;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "| Feature             | Python                | R                             |\n",
        "|--------------------|----------------------|------------------------------|\n",
        "| Install package     | `pip install seaborn` | `install.packages(\"ggplot2\")` |\n",
        "| Load package        | `import seaborn`      | `library(ggplot2)`            |\n",
        "| When install needed | Once                  | Once                          |\n",
        "| When load needed    | Every session         | Every session                 |\n",
        "| Keyword difference  | `import`              | `library()`                   |\n",
        "\n",
        "::: callout-note\n",
        "## Importing Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "Importing functions from packages is the same as importing functions from modules. The syntax remains identical; only the source (module or package) changes.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "### Creating and Accessing Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  Create a Directory: create a folder that will act as the package root.\n",
        "2.  Add Modules: Inside the directory, add Python files (modules). Each module can contain related functions or classes.\n",
        "3.  Add **init**.py: add an **init**.py file to the directory. This file tells Python that the directory should be treated as a package.\n",
        "4.  Create Sub-packages (Optional): One can organize code further by creating subdirectories with their own **init**.py files.\n",
        "5.  Import Modules: Modules or functions inside the package can be imported using dot notation, for example: `from mypackage.module1 import greet`\n",
        "\n",
        "**Example**\n",
        "\n",
        "In this example, we create a package called math_operations to organize mathematical functions. The package contains two modules:\n",
        "\n",
        "-   basic: addition and subtraction\n",
        "-   advanced: multiplication and division\n",
        "\n",
        "First create a new folder named math_operations. Then create an empty `__init__.py` file in the math_operations folder. Create modules `basic.py` and `advanced.py` with following code:"
      ],
      "id": "4538eb86"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# basic.py\n",
        "\n",
        "def add(a, b):\n",
        "    return a + b\n",
        "  \n",
        "def subtract(a, b):\n",
        "    return a - b  "
      ],
      "id": "2f18b86d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# advanced.py\n",
        "\n",
        "def multiply(a, b):\n",
        "    return a * b\n",
        "  \n",
        "def divide(a, b):\n",
        "    return a / b\n"
      ],
      "id": "b1c1a3d8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The folder structure of your package should look like this:\n",
        "\n",
        "``` text\n",
        "math_operations/\n",
        "├── __init__.py\n",
        "├── basic.py\n",
        "└── advanced.py\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Now we can import functions from the package and use them in our program."
      ],
      "id": "3752252a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from math_operations import add, subtract, multiply, divide\n",
        "\n",
        "# Perform basic operations\n",
        "print(add(5, 3))\n",
        "print(subtract(10, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Perform advanced operations\n",
        "print(multiply(4, 2))\n",
        "print(divide(10, 2))"
      ],
      "id": "8aa2bf3a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As you can see our package works perfectly.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Popular Python Packages \\\\ Libraries for Data Analysis and Data Science\n",
        "\n",
        "Python provides a rich ecosystem of packages for data analysis, statistical computing, and visualization. Some of the most widely used packages include:\n",
        "\n",
        "-   **NumPy** – Provides support for numerical computing, multidimensional arrays, and mathematical operations.\n",
        "-   **Pandas** – Offers data structures such as DataFrames for data manipulation, cleaning, and analysis.\n",
        "-   **Matplotlib** – A comprehensive library for creating static, interactive, and animated visualizations.\n",
        "-   **Statsmodels** – Provides tools for statistical modeling, hypothesis testing, regression analysis, and time series analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "These packages form the foundation of many data science workflows in Python and are conceptually similar to several commonly used R packages for data analysis and statistical modeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple comparison table could also be useful:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Area                 | Python Packages | R Packages      |\n",
        "|:---------------------|:----------------|:----------------|\n",
        "| Numerical computing  | NumPy           | base R          |\n",
        "| Data manipulation    | Pandas          | dplyr           |\n",
        "| Data visualization   | Matplotlib      | ggplot2         |\n",
        "| Statistical modeling | Statsmodels     | stats, forecast |\n",
        "\n",
        "The following sections provide a more detailed discussion of these packages, including their key features, common functions, and practical applications in data analysis and data science workflows."
      ],
      "id": "559c38ff"
    }
  ],
